        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "For the invention and realization of optical lattice clocks."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B003",
    "B045"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks solve a central experimental problem: how to interrogate an atomic transition with extraordinary precision while suppressing shifts from atomic motion. Katori and Ye developed complementary approaches that established the method, and its performance has been reproduced and advanced across laboratories. The clocks have become tools for precision measurement and tests of fundamental physics, with their scientific value already demonstrated.",
    "case_against": "The boundary of the achievement needs care: the prize should recognize the clock invention and its realization, rather than the full subsequent development of optical frequency standards. Later experimental advances involved many groups, so the citation and attribution should stay focused on the foundational work of Katori and Ye.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that circuit QED is a mature foundational platform in its own right, independent of whether fault-tolerant quantum computers are achieved, and notes the clear attribution of the optical lattice clock advances to Katori and Ye.",
        "response": "I agree that circuit QED should be assessed on its established physics rather than on the future of quantum computing. The clocks have an especially direct case: their measurement capability is already established and useful, so their recognition does not depend on a future application reaching maturity."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth emphasizes that squeezed light has produced measurable sensitivity improvements in operating gravitational-wave detectors, while noting that source development and implementation involved other teams, including Roman Schnabel.",
        "response": "That deployment is a strong demonstration of quantum optics improving a major instrument, and the attribution deserves careful review. I would still place the clocks ahead because their two complementary founding contributions are comparatively well defined, and their precision measurement impact extends beyond a single detector application."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The precise boundary between the original optical lattice clock advances and the later development of optical frequency standards needs to be stated clearly.",
      "The role of subsequent groups in improving clock performance should be weighed without obscuring the foundational contributions of Katori and Ye."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No connection to Katori or Ye is apparent from the supplied profile. The profile notes WACQT overlap with fellow member Göran Johansson, but no connection to this achievement."
  }
}
